1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,793
The charge came early and it was explicit: the group had measured the wrong thing.

2
00:00:04,793 --> 00:00:10,622
A visiting examiner glanced at their table and asked what the numbers could mean.

3
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:14,232
“You count arrivals,” she said.

4
00:00:14,232 --> 00:00:22,176
“You cannot see comprehension.” There was a lack of wisdom in the early draft, and they knew it.

5
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:27,345
The criticism was not commercial; it was about territory.

6
00:00:27,345 --> 00:00:34,039
But their numbers did not increase, and that was the first sign of trouble.

7
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:38,829
A sensible answer would separate retention from recognition.

8
00:00:38,829 --> 00:00:46,387
A student who can recite a definition may still fail to recognize the same idea in a new context.

9
00:00:46,387 --> 00:00:54,416
Memory, they wrote, is not a certificate; it is a presence that decays unless it is used.

10
00:00:54,416 --> 00:01:01,560
The decrease was visible in their own data, and the meaning of that line was the catch.

11
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,475
The obstacle was perception.

12
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:18,268
A skilled reader could penetrate a vague sentence at a glance, and a beginner could not; an oval diagram of their results made that difference noticeable, and her finger followed it while she talked.

13
00:01:18,268 --> 00:01:24,022
“You should instruct with an illustrative example,” Professor Zhao said.

14
00:01:24,022 --> 00:01:30,117
“Show the glimpse of understanding, not the purchase of a book.”

15
00:01:30,117 --> 00:01:33,436
They began to oppose the easy number.

16
00:01:33,436 --> 00:01:44,999
A gifted singer, a rental clerk, and a boy with a rifle on a poster all appeared in the same week's notes, and none of them could qualify as evidence.

17
00:01:44,999 --> 00:01:58,247
“We must greet the sceptic,” Maya said, “and not be bothered by her.” It was a sophisticated position, and it took sophistication to defend it without sparkle.

18
00:01:58,247 --> 00:02:06,261
Other volunteers agreed; a kid from the first year asked the sharpest question of the evening.

19
00:02:06,261 --> 00:02:14,270
So they rebuilt the study around two questions: what would a reader remember, and what could a reader do?

20
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:22,454
An invention of their own — a seven-line test — became the new instrument, and its performance improved the whole file.

21
00:02:22,454 --> 00:02:28,298
They were readily aware of its limits, and they did not regret stating them.

22
00:02:28,298 --> 00:02:33,623
“Do not let the outside world intrude on the meaning,” Lin wrote.

23
00:02:33,623 --> 00:02:46,191
“Enlighten it instead.” Later, a fresh teacher asked whether the test prevented guessing; the honest answer was that it did not, and they wrote that down too.

24
00:02:46,191 --> 00:02:49,021
“Yeah,” Maya said.

25
00:02:49,021 --> 00:02:53,331
“Turn the page and start again.”
